The '''Asahi Line''' ({{lang|ja|朝日線}}) is a [[rapid transit]] line of the [[Okami Metro]] system in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 3''', it is the third-oldest subway line in the city, having opened in 1965. The line is coloured '''lime green''' (#32CD32) on system maps and route diagrams. It runs from {{station|Hozumi-Oiwake}} in the north to {{station|Tobe Kaigan}} in the south, spanning a total of 21 stations over a distance of {{convert|17.3|km|abbr=on}}. The Asahi Line serves as a critical north–south artery connecting the residential districts of northern Aoi Ward with the coastal port area of Tobe Ward.

The line was originally constructed to serve the eastern districts of Okami, linking the growing residential neighbourhoods of Hozumi-Oiwake to the coastal Tobe Kaigan area. Over the decades, the Asahi Line has become one of the busiest corridors in the Okami Metro network, carrying commuters, students, and port workers across the city. With 13 interchange stations, it provides connections to the {{OMSN|M|00}} [[Miyoshi Line]], {{OMSN|T|00}} [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]], {{OMSN|A|00}} [[Asahi Line]], {{OMSN|C|00}} [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]], and {{OMSN|I|00}} [[Imaichi Line]], among others.

Trains on the Asahi Line operate with 8-car formations using the [[#Rolling stock|5000 series]] Type B rolling stock, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] and introduced in 2018. The line is electrified at 1500&nbsp;V DC via [[overhead line]] and uses [[standard gauge]] (1435&nbsp;mm) track. The maximum operating speed is {{convert|100|km/h|abbr=on}}, making it one of the faster lines in the Okami Metro network. The combination of high speed and frequent service makes the Asahi Line particularly important for long-distance commuters travelling from the northern suburbs to the port district.

The Asahi Line is designated as Line 3 in the Okami Metro numbering system and carries the line code '''A'''. The line's 13 interchange stations make it one of the most connected lines in the network, providing seamless transfers to the east–west [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] at Asahimachi Chuo, as well as connections to the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] at Tobe Kaigan. This high degree of connectivity has contributed to the line's consistently high ridership and its role as a key backbone of the Okami Metro system.

== History ==

The Asahi Line was opened in 1965 as the third subway line in the Okami Metro system, following the earlier Miyoshi and Tozai Lines. The initial segment was built to serve the eastern districts of Okami, providing a vital connection between the residential areas of Hozumi-Oiwake in the north and the coastal Tobe Kaigan area in the south. The opening of the line was a major milestone in the city's post-war transit development, reflecting the rapid population growth and urbanisation of Okami's eastern wards during the 1950s and 1960s.

During the 1970s and 1980s, the line was extended further south to serve the growing port district of Tobe. This extension was driven by the significant industrial and commercial development of the Tobe coastline, which had become one of Okaiken's busiest port areas. The extension brought the line to its current southern terminus at Tobe Kaigan, directly serving the port facilities and the surrounding residential neighbourhoods that had developed to house port workers and their families.

The Asahi Line connects with multiple other lines at key hubs throughout the city. At its northern terminus, Hozumi-Oiwake provides interchange with the Miyoshi Line (M) and Tozai Line (T), as well as the OR Main Line and OR Okami Loop Line. At Asahimachi Chuo, passengers can transfer to the Chūō Line (C) and Imaichi Line (I). The southern terminus at Tobe Kaigan offers connections to the Miyoshi Line (M39), while the northern section connects to the [[Akitetsu Akita Line]] via the [[Akitetsu Asahi Line Connector]] at Hiyori-cho. These interchange points make the Asahi Line a critical backbone of the Okami Metro network, facilitating cross-city travel for hundreds of thousands of passengers daily.

In 2018, the line received its current fleet of 5000 series Type B trains, replacing the older 1000 series rolling stock that had served the line since its early years. The 5000 series trains, manufactured by Okami Heavy Industries, feature modern amenities including air conditioning, accessibility features, and improved passenger information systems. The 8-car formations provide sufficient capacity for the line's high ridership, particularly during peak commuting hours.

The introduction of the 5000 series marked a significant modernisation of the Asahi Line, bringing it in line with the latest standards in urban rail transit. The new trains feature regenerative braking systems that recover energy during deceleration, reducing the line's overall energy consumption. Additionally, the 5000 series incorporates advanced safety systems, including automatic train protection (ATP) and improved crashworthiness standards. The trains' exterior livery features the line's signature lime green colour, making them easily identifiable on the network.

Throughout its history, the Asahi Line has undergone several phases of renovation and upgrading. Station platforms have been progressively modernised with the installation of [[platform screen doors]], improved lighting, and accessibility features such as elevators and tactile paving for visually impaired passengers. These improvements have been part of a broader effort by the Okami Metropolitan Bureau of Transportation to make the metro system more accessible and safe for all passengers.

== Route description ==

The Asahi Line runs in a generally north–south direction through the eastern districts of Okami. From its northern terminus at Hozumi-Oiwake in Aoi Ward, the line travels south through the wards of Chiyoda, Chuo, Tama, Higashi, and Minami before reaching its southern terminus at Tobe Kaigan in Tobe Ward. The route passes through a diverse mix of residential, commercial, and industrial areas, reflecting the varied character of Okami's eastern districts.

The northern section of the line, between Hozumi-Oiwake and Asahimachi Chuo, serves primarily residential neighbourhoods with high-density apartment complexes and several large parks. This section is particularly busy during morning and evening rush hours as commuters travel from the residential areas of Aoi and Chiyoda wards toward the city centre. Stations along this section feature elevated and underground configurations, with the northernmost stations utilising deep-level tunnel construction to navigate the hilly terrain of northern Okami.

The central section, between Asahimachi Chuo and Takagawa, passes through commercial districts with significant office and retail development. This section includes some of the busiest stations on the line, particularly Asahimachi Chuo, which serves as a major interchange with the Chūō and Imaichi Lines. The area around Asahimachi Chuo is one of Okami's principal commercial centres, with numerous department stores, office buildings, and cultural institutions.

The southern section, from Takagawa to Tobe Kaigan, transitions through mixed-use areas before reaching the port district at the line's southern end. This section serves a mix of industrial workers, port employees, and residents of the growing waterfront communities. The Tobe Kaigan terminus is particularly important as it provides direct access to the port facilities, ferry terminals, and the growing number of commercial and recreational developments along the Tobe coastline. The southern extension, completed in stages during the 1970s and 1980s, was instrumental in supporting the economic development of the Tobe port area.

